in other world news dozens of rohingya  refugees feared drowned while trying to  swim to malaysian shores  have been found alive the coast guard  says they were hiding in bushes on a  small island  all have been detained as malaysia does  not recognize refugee status  more than 700 000 rohingya fled myanmar  after a military crackdown in 2017  most are living in camps in bangladesh  let's get more from florence lui who's  in kuala lumpur forest  florence so a lucky escape for these  refugees tell us about what happened and  how they were found  well yes so initially they were thought  to have drowned because only one person  from that group made it to shore on  saturday night and he was detained by  police and authorities launched a search  and rescue operation on saturday evening  and into sunday as well  but now authorities say that the rest of  the group 26 of them  were found hiding in bushes on an eyelet  just off  langkawi island in the north west of  peninsula malaysia  and they were found late on sunday now  in addition to the 26 people who were  found  there was also another man who was  rescued by fishermen  at sea now authorities say they have  detained all  27 of them and 28 including the man who  made it to shore on saturday night  now a senior coast guard official told  reuters news agency  that these people nearly 30 of them are  believed to have been smuggled into the  country  on a smaller boat and he believed that  they  likely traveled to malaysia on a bigger  ship  from bangladesh as ship that could carry  about hundreds of  other rohingya refugees now these men  have now these people have now been  detained they include  men women and children they've been  detained in addition authorities say  they've also arrested two  other rohingya who are suspected of  being involved in the  trafficking or people smuggling off this  group of people into  malaysia now malaysia as a country does  not recognize refugees so it means that  those who make it here don't have any  legal status they can't work  they can't send their children to school  but malaysia is still considered a  favorite destination a sort of safe  haven  because it's a muslim-majority country  and  the mainly muslim rohingya feel safe  here despite not having any legal status  but that is slowly changing  now last month malaysian authorities  detained nearly 270  rohingya rohingya refugees they were  found to vent at the country  their boat was damaged malaysia in the  past has sent rohingya refugees back  out to sea but the authorities say they  couldn't do so because this boat was  damaged  now the prime minister has said malaysia  is no longer able to accept any more  refugees it's already struggling to cope  with an economy that's been battered by  the pandemic  thank you very much for that florence  louis live for us in  kuala lumpur
 

